---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const a = appBySlug('shopify-email')!;
const b = appBySlug('privy')!;
const path = '/compare/shopify-email-vs-privy';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-privy');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-privy');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Shopify Email or Privy for cart recovery?', answer: 'Keep recovery native when the available Shopify Messaging workflow meets your needs. Consider Privy when you want the team managing onsite signup offers to also manage welcome messages and recovery. Compare the actual forms, event eligibility and offer rules before moving send ownership.' },
  { question: 'Can I keep Privy forms and Shopify recovery?', answer: 'Treat form capture and recovery sending as separate responsibilities. Verify how each form records email permission and whether Shopify sees the intended subscription state. Disable overlapping welcome and recovery sends; a captured address alone does not prove eligibility for either channel.' },
  { question: 'Should the cart reminder add another discount?', answer: 'First inspect the offer already promised at signup. Test its validity, expiry and combination rules in checkout. A reminder can repeat an existing valid offer or explain delivery without introducing a deeper discount.' },
  { question: 'How should I switch recovery senders?', answer: 'Inventory forms, welcome flows, recovery flows and outstanding offers. Test the replacement with authorized contacts, pause old entry and inspect pending sends before enabling it. Keep a rollback owner and preserve permission and suppression history.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Decide who owns the journey from signup offer to cart reminder, without sending two discounts for the same purchase.</p>
    <div class="mt-10"><TldrBox picks={[
      { label: 'Native recovery', tool: a.name, reason: 'Evaluate the available Shopify Messaging templates before adding another sender.' },
      { label: 'Capture and follow-up', tool: b.name, reason: 'Consider onsite offers, welcome messages and cart recovery as one operating workflow.' },
      { label: 'Decision rule', tool: 'One offer owner', reason: 'Track the promise made at signup through the eventual checkout.' },
    ]} /></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Compare against the current native workflow</h2>
      <p>This route uses the familiar Shopify Email name; current Shopify documentation calls the app Shopify Messaging. Its recovery templates distinguish browsing, cart abandonment before checkout, and checkout abandonment. Email, SMS and WhatsApp are documented channels, subject to account eligibility and availability. Native checkout recovery email does not count toward the email send allowance; texts and WhatsApp messages are charged. Start with the <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">Shopify automation guide</a> and the actual options in your store.</p>
      <p>Shopify also documents welcome-discount automations and Shopify Forms. Privy therefore needs to justify a different operating workflow, rather than merely the existence of a popup or welcome email. Keep recovery native if your team can maintain the needed message, audience and offer there. Fix payment errors or unexpected delivery charges at checkout before expecting another sender to improve recovery.</p>
      <h2>Where Privy changes the job</h2>
      <p>Privy's <a href="https://www.privy.com/list-growth">list-growth overview</a> brings onsite displays, welcome codes and cart reminder emails together. Its <a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/new-features-2025">Flows announcement</a> documents email and SMS steps, behavioral triggers and audience filters. Those capabilities merit evaluation when one small team owns both subscriber acquisition and follow-up. Neither page proves that an anonymous visitor is reachable, that your existing forms sync every needed field, or that every feature is included in your account.</p>
      <p>Map each live display to its promise: which audience sees it, what permission it records, which code it issues, and which welcome flow follows. Then map cart recovery separately. A visitor who requests a signup code and later starts checkout can qualify for two messages with different purposes. One should deliver the promised offer; the other should help complete the purchase. They should not compete by inventing a second, better offer.</p>
      <h2>Three operating choices</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Keep capture and recovery native:</strong> suitable when the available Shopify form and automation meet the job. Your acceptance test is whether the real signup produces the intended welcome message and checkout recovery, with correct eligibility and purchase exits.</li>
        <li><strong>Use Privy for capture, keep native recovery:</strong> useful to evaluate when the display is the main reason to adopt Privy. Verify subscription-state transfer and welcome ownership. Keeping Privy recovery inactive avoids assuming that connecting a form also transfers recovery responsibility.</li>
        <li><strong>Move capture and recovery to Privy:</strong> evaluate when the same team needs to manage displays and follow-up there. Pause overlapping Shopify welcome and recovery automations deliberately. Shared store data is not proof that separate send queues coordinate.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For each choice, record the owner of welcome email, cart email, checkout email and any text. Name the person who can pause each sender. Keep email and text permission histories distinct, including opt-outs and source records; do not turn an order phone number into text permission. Validate supplier and market requirements for the intended audience.</p>
      <h2>Proposed test: follow one signup promise to checkout</h2>
      <p>The following is an acceptance plan, not an executed store test. Use authorized test contacts and retain the event, eligibility and send logs.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Submit a live-form test with an empty cart. Inspect the subscription record, welcome sender and issued code. Repeat with a returning subscriber to see whether the form issues another code or starts another welcome flow.</li>
        <li>Add a product but do not start checkout. Check which cart trigger is recorded and whether either sender queues a reminder. Then start checkout and inspect whether cart-stage work is superseded or remains pending.</li>
        <li>Open the welcome offer and recovery link on mobile. Check the intended variant, quantity, code expiry, minimum spend, excluded items and combination settings. Change the basket and repeat; a preview alone cannot establish checkout behavior.</li>
        <li>Purchase while a reminder waits, then inspect both queues. Repeat after an opt-out. Also test an out-of-stock variant so the reminder does not promise an unavailable item or create a dead-end return.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If signup and recovery both send, assess the sequence against the promise map rather than banning every pair of messages. A code delivery followed by useful checkout assistance may be intentional. Two welcome codes or two recovery offers require correction. Choose delays from the actual buying cycle and observed overlap; there is no universal minute-by-minute ladder or contact cap established by this comparison.</p>
      <h2>Price the offer as well as the software</h2>
      <p>Hypothetical example: a $438 basket at a 44% full-price gross margin has $245.28 product cost and $192.72 gross profit. A 17% discount removes $74.46 from revenue. Discounted revenue of $363.54 minus unchanged product cost leaves $118.26 gross profit, before fulfillment, payment fees, returns and messaging. Multiplying the original profit by 83% would incorrectly reduce product cost along with revenue.</p>
      <p>If the welcome offer already promises that 17% discount, compare recovery with that offer against recovery without an additional concession. Do not attribute all discounted purchases to a new cart flow. Where feasible, use a randomized comparison with a sample and duration suited to your traffic; reconcile Shopify order IDs, discounts and refunds across senders. Count incremental profit rather than adding two dashboards' attributed revenue.</p>
      <p>Request current quotes for the actual contact count, email volume, text destinations and required features. Include display management, migration work and staff time. A form's signup rate is insufficient: inspect permission quality, subsequent purchases and offer cost. This page makes no fixed price, recovery-rate or ranking claim.</p>
      <h2>Cut over without losing outstanding promises</h2>
      <p>Save the current form rules, offer text, active codes, suppression records and flow settings before switching. Rebuild the replacement inactive and run the proposed tests. At cutover, pause old entry and inspect delayed work; disabling new entry may not cancel messages already queued. Confirm the supplier's actual queue behavior rather than assuming it.</p>
      <p>Keep existing promised codes valid for their stated terms or provide an explicit customer-facing resolution. Do not replace every form and sender simultaneously if that would make failures impossible to diagnose. Name a rollback owner who can stop the new flow, resolve its queued messages and restore the chosen old flow without enabling duplicate recovery.</p>
      <p>Sequenzy can remain a later supplier to evaluate if a separate lifecycle requirement emerges. This comparison does not establish it as a necessary extra layer or as a solution for Shopify and Privy queue coordination. Require evidence for the specific integration and exclusions before adding a third sender.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
